body:
What would be the url when using the router to navigate? 

If I use 
`[routerLink]="['/test', { f: '[724,692]' }]"`
the target url is **/test;f=[724,692]** (fine)

But if I navigate using this.router.navigate (code bellow), the target url is **/test?f=[724,692]** (it seems wrong)

```
var route = '/test';
var params = {
            f:  this.criarParametroDeFiltrosDeCategoria(),
            fv: this.criarParametroDeFiltrosValor(),
            o:  this.criarParametroOrdem(),
            pg: this.pagina,
        };

        var navigationExtras = { queryParams: params };
        this.router.navigate([route], navigationExtras);
```

Reading angular documentation on [https://angular.io/docs/ts/latest/guide/router.html](https://angular.io/docs/ts/latest/guide/router.html), I could find what is the expected target url .

I am having the same problem, and it's also a security issue in the case of user supplied data as it will break routes, so I'm thinking this should be treated with a bit more urgency. Any progress on this?

As a workaround, and to speed things up, I _think_ that one could change the `encode` function in [`modules/@angular/router/src/url_tree.ts:344`](https://github.com/angular/angular/blob/a343a8e1c2673ea19ae9a98ebd0f04329af5f718/modules/%40angular/router/src/url_tree.ts#L344) to:

``` typescript
export function encode(s: string): string {
  return encodeURIComponent(s).replace(/[!'()*]/g, (c) => {
    // Also encode !, ', (, ), and *
    return '%' + c.charCodeAt(0).toString(16);
  });
}
```

I hope this is the right place in the code! It may also be needed for query params, and even other places. Proper decoding for params may also be needed when params are injected into a component.

Essentially, you want to be looking for

``` html
<a [routerLink]="['foo', '(bar)']"></a>
```

to be turned into

``` html
<a href="/foo/%2528bar%2529"></a>
```

The reasoning for this is explained [here](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/encodeURIComponent#Description) (see last paragraph in the section called "Description").

I was going to submit a PR for this, but [this legal nonsense](https://cla.developers.google.com/about/google-individual?csw=1) ruined it for me.

**For anyone needing a workaround**, use the following pipe:

``` typescript
import { Pipe, PipeTransform } from '@angular/core';

@Pipe({
  name: 'encodeURIComponent'
})

export class EncodeUriComponentPipe implements PipeTransform {
  transform(uriComponent: string): string {
    return encodeURIComponent(uriComponent).replace(/[!'()*]/g, (c) => {
      // Also encode !, ', (, ), and *
      return '%' + c.charCodeAt(0).toString(16);
    });
  }
}
```

and then

``` html
<a [routerLink]="['foo', ('(bar)' | encodeURIComponent)]">your link</a>
```

That fixes it for me, but it would be nice to see this adopted in Angular (if this is the correct solution, that is, as I may well be wrong).

I've tried a really wild solution as I read the comment from @dchacke.

As a workaround what about patching encodeURIComponent?
```typescript
const encode = encodeURIComponent;
window['encodeURIComponent'] = (component: string) => {
  return encode(component).replace(/[!'()*]/g, (c) => {
    // Also encode !, ', (, ), and *
    return '%' + c.charCodeAt(0).toString(16);
  });
}
```
It seems working for me, but I cannot predict the side effects of patching a global function.
